      "title": "Leadership Institute Student Fellows Program",
      "description": "The Leadership Institute Student Fellows Program | Institutes and Centers Skip to content Leadership Institute Student Fellows Program READ ARTICLE Close Search Apply Request Info Make a Gift Academics Admissions & Aid Student Life About Faith and Reason Program and Major List All Programs Humanities & Social Sciences Natural & Applied Sciences Professional Programs Theology & Philosophy Online Institutes and Centers Center for Criminal Justice, Law, & Ethics Institute for Science & Health Leadership Institute Student Fellows Priestly Discernment Program All Institutes & Centers Resources Registrar Academic Catalog Academic Advising & Support Academic Calendar St. John Paul II Library Study Abroad in Austria Austria Program Home Summer Experience Information for Parents Apply Request Info Visit Admission General Admissions Undergraduate Graduate Visit Franciscan Online Programs Transfer Secure Your Enrollment New Student Orientation Austria Study Abroad St. John Paul II Library Tuition & Financial Aid Costs & Fees Tuition & Financial Aid Scholarship International Students Undergraduate Graduate Apply Request Info Visit Living Your Faith Chapel Schedule & Info Places to Pray The Franciscans, TOR Housing & Dining Residence Life Households Off-Campus Housing Dining Student Opportunities Student Clubs Outreach Student Employment Baron Athletics Student Resources Student Handbook Wellness Center Apply Request Info Visit Alumni & Giving Alumni News & Events Step in Faith Annual Fund Legacy Giving Ways to Give Why FUS? FUS at a Glance Mission, Vision, & Charism Our History Strategic Planning Resources Campus Map Campus Safety Travel and Accommodations News & Events University News Upcoming Events Franciscan Magazine Apply Request Info Visit Academics Admissions & Aid Student Life About Faith and Reason Back Home Connect Program and Major List All Programs Humanities & Social Sciences Natural & Applied Sciences Professional Programs Theology & Philosophy Online Institutes and Centers Center for Criminal Justice, Law, & Ethics Institute for Science & Health Leadership Institute Student Fellows Priestly Discernment Program All Institutes & Centers Resources Registrar Academic Catalog Academic Advising & Support Academic Calendar St. John Paul II Library Study Abroad in Austria Austria Program Home Summer Experience Information for Parents Connect Admission General Admissions Undergraduate Graduate Visit Franciscan Online Programs Transfer Secure Your Enrollment New Student Orientation Austria Study Abroad St. John Paul II Library Tuition & Financial Aid Costs & Fees Tuition & Financial Aid Scholarship International Students Undergraduate Graduate Connect Living Your Faith Chapel Schedule & Info Places to Pray The Franciscans, TOR Housing & Dining Residence Life Households Off-Campus Housing Dining Student Opportunities Student Clubs Outreach Student Employment Baron Athletics Student Resources Student Handbook Wellness Center Connect Alumni & Giving Alumni News & Events Step in Faith Annual Fund Legacy Giving Ways to Give Why FUS? FUS at a Glance Mission, Vision, & Charism Our History Strategic Planning Resources Campus Map Campus Safety Travel and Accommodations News & Events University News Upcoming Events Franciscan Magazine The Need The Mission The Program Events Application and Admission The Need America needs leaders. Desperately. It needs leaders who understand the dignity of the human person, the sanctity of life, and role of faith in shaping the social order. It also needs leaders who understand both natural law and divine law, who have been schooled in the virtues, and formed in the truth. And it needs leaders with practical competence who are capable of making the hard choices, doing the right thing, and sacrificing themselves for the good of others and the nation. It doesn’t, however, just need those leaders in Washington. It needs them everywhere, in every walk of life, transforming both Church and culture as doctors and lawyers, journalists and corporate executives, artists and academics. That is the unique vocation of the laity: to transform the social order from within, to be salt and light and leaven in the world. The Mission Through the Leadership Institute Student Fellows Program, Franciscan University of Steubenville is committed to raising up a new generation of Catholic leaders who can do just that, recognizing as its former president, Father Michael Scanlan, TOR, once asked, “If Franciscan University doesn’t produce leaders for tomorrow, who will?” Founded in 2011 as the Center for Leadership, the Student Fellows program equips a select group of Franciscan undergraduate students for the unique challenges of leadership in the 21st century. The Student Fellows Program works to inspire students to fulfill their particular vocations to “renew and constantly perfect the temporal order” by fostering virtues, imparting knowledge, and cultivating practical skills essential for effective, lifelong Christian leadership. The Program The Leadership Institute Student Fellows Program is a three-year formation program open to Franciscan University students with a demonstrated commitment to service and leadership. Student Fellows participate in weekly seminars during sophomore and junior years, for guided discussions based on texts critical to their formation as young leaders.  Senior year students complete a leadership workshop tied to their on-campus leadership position. Through readings, reflections, self-assessment, and coaching, the program helps students understand and embrace their unique personal vocation as both a present reality and a future call to be discovered. Student Fellows are required to step out of the classroom and exercise leadership as an officer of a student organization, a coordinator of a household, a resident assistant, a leader of a mission team, or another student leadership position on campus. Student Fellows select a trained vocation coach from among faculty and staff, enabling each to receive coaching as they work to gain deeper awareness of their own personal vocations and build strategies for effectively developing themselves as leaders. The program facilitates engagement with Career Services, providing practical tools from resumé writing and vocational assessment to etiquette and career networking.  Among the tools employed is the Clifton StrengthsFinder assessment to enable students to measure their talents and better understand their natural patterns of behaving. Student Fellows are required to complete an internship or work experience related to their occupational interests. Events Leadership Mass An annual Mass offered for the initiation of the students in the Center for Leadership. Student Leadership Conferences The annual Student Leadership Conference provides an opportunity for students from across campus to gather to learn, reflect and pray together as young leaders. The conference equips students to meet the challenges of leading their peers and others, helping them grow as effective Christian leaders. Conference speakers have included theologian and author Fr. Peter Ryan, S.J.; Stephen Giordano of Day One Group; Dr. Scott and Kimberly Hahn; Fr. Dave Pivonka, TOR; and Mark Hart, executive director of LifeTeen. Application and Admission Freshmen may apply to the Student Fellows Program after they have completed at least one full semester at Franciscan.  Transfer students who expect to complete at least three years of study at Franciscan may apply during their first semester. Additional information on the Leadership Institute Student Fellows Program and an application are circulated to eligible students each semester. Questions regarding the Student Fellows leadership program should be directed to [email protected] Previous tab Next tab Center for Leadership Staff David Schmiesing Schmiesing was appointed as vice president of Student Life in September 2006. He graduated summa cum laude from Franciscan University in 1992 with a BA in history and a minor in mathematics. That same year he earned an Ohio secondary teaching certificate. In 1999, he completed an MS in education at Franciscan University. Read More He has served Franciscan University in a variety of leadership positions, most notably as associate director of Conferences (1997), director of Business Services (1998-2002), and associate dean of Student Development (2002-2006). He has a long history of involvement with students, overseeing student leadership development and other programs that promote involvement in campus life and social and spiritual development. Mr. Schmiesing also serves as the director of the Franciscan University Center for Leadership. Ronald J. McNamara McNamara serves as coordinator of Student Leadership Development at Franciscan University of Steubenville, drawing on his extensive public policy and diplomatic experience in Washington, D.C., Vienna, Austria, and throughout Eastern Europe. A graduate of The George Washington University, with a BA in political science, Mr. McNamara held numerous leadership and supervisory positions during his career in public service on Capitol Hill, which spanned over three decades. Josh Miller Joshua’s life work is about helping persons identify how God has uniquely designed and called them so that they can flourish and help sanctify the world. This mission is central to his role as husband, father, friend and in his professional career in his roles as coach, trainer, speaker and writer. Read More Much of Joshua’s work centers on drawing out the achievement stories of other people and helping them see the unique patterns of created design in them. His long standing desire to understand and help people develop their unique giftedness led to an MA & Ph.D. in Philosophy of the Human Person. He continues to effectively integrate learning from this study into his coaching and consulting practices. He is a co-developer of MCORE, the first on-line assessment that blends a person’s own achievement stories with established psychometric constructs. For the last 20 years he has applied his expertise in a variety of applications, including executive search, organizational development, and talent management. Joshua also helped build The Center for Leadership at Franciscan University of Steubenville where he currently serves as Coordinator for the First Year Program and the Vocation Coach Program. In recent years Joshua has become increasingly concerned that the Church’s rich teaching personal vocation has been neglected. As a response to this concern, Joshua co-founded Inscape in 2016 with Luke Burgis, which is devoted to fostering a culture of personal vocation in the Church and for the renewal of Culture. He is the co-author with Luke Burgis of Unrepeatable: Cultivating the Unique Calling of Every Person . Joshua and his wife Brooke of 20 years are joyfully Catholic and have six dear children. Gallery Discovering Your Personal Vocation Interning with the Congressman A Reception to Remember About Franciscan Franciscan University of Steubenville is no ordinary university, and a Franciscan education is no ordinary education. Rather, it’s an education as rigorous and demanding as it is faithful—an education that challenges you intellectually, forms you professionally, and feeds you spiritually. Apply Inquire Visit Give Our Schools All Programs School of Professional Programs School of Theology and Philosophy School of Natural and Applied Sciences School of Humanities and Social Sciences Franciscan Community Student Life Household Life Residence Life Student Clubs Athletics Alumni & Friends Steubenville Conferences Resources Career Opportunities The Port Visit, Amenities, & Directions Campus Store Campus Safety Title IX Resources Hazing Prevention Student Handbook Admissions Undergraduate Graduate Online Transfer International Tuition & Financial Aid © Franciscan University of Steubenville Contact Us Privacy Policy Directions Nondiscrimination Statement Consumer Information Terms & Conditions Go to Top Apply Inquire Visit Programs Give"
    },
    "https://franciscan.edu/make-a-deposit/": {
      "title": "UndergraduateStudent Enrollment Checklist",
      "description": "Deposit & Admitted Student Steps | Franciscan University of Steubenville Skip to content Close Search Apply Request Info Make a Gift Academics Admissions & Aid Student Life About Faith and Reason Program and Major List All Programs Humanities & Social Sciences Natural & Applied Sciences Professional Programs Theology & Philosophy Online Institutes and Centers Center for Criminal Justice, Law, & Ethics Institute for Science & Health Leadership Institute Student Fellows Priestly Discernment Program All Institutes & Centers Resources Registrar Academic Catalog Academic Advising & Support Academic Calendar St. John Paul II Library Study Abroad in Austria Austria Program Home Summer Experience Information for Parents Apply Request Info Visit Admission General Admissions Undergraduate Graduate Visit Franciscan Online Programs Transfer Secure Your Enrollment New Student Orientation Austria Study Abroad St. John Paul II Library Tuition & Financial Aid Costs & Fees Tuition & Financial Aid Scholarship International Students Undergraduate Graduate Apply Request Info Visit Living Your Faith Chapel Schedule & Info Places to Pray The Franciscans, TOR Housing & Dining Residence Life Households Off-Campus Housing Dining Student Opportunities Student Clubs Outreach Student Employment Baron Athletics Student Resources Student Handbook Wellness Center Apply Request Info Visit Alumni & Giving Alumni News & Events Step in Faith Annual Fund Legacy Giving Ways to Give Why FUS? FUS at a Glance Mission, Vision, & Charism Our History Strategic Planning Resources Campus Map Campus Safety Travel and Accommodations News & Events University News Upcoming Events Franciscan Magazine Apply Request Info Visit Academics Admissions & Aid Student Life About Faith and Reason Back Home Connect Program and Major List All Programs Humanities & Social Sciences Natural & Applied Sciences Professional Programs Theology & Philosophy Online Institutes and Centers Center for Criminal Justice, Law, & Ethics Institute for Science & Health Leadership Institute Student Fellows Priestly Discernment Program All Institutes & Centers Resources Registrar Academic Catalog Academic Advising & Support Academic Calendar St. John Paul II Library Study Abroad in Austria Austria Program Home Summer Experience Information for Parents Connect Admission General Admissions Undergraduate Graduate Visit Franciscan Online Programs Transfer Secure Your Enrollment New Student Orientation Austria Study Abroad St. John Paul II Library Tuition & Financial Aid Costs & Fees Tuition & Financial Aid Scholarship International Students Undergraduate Graduate Connect Living Your Faith Chapel Schedule & Info Places to Pray The Franciscans, TOR Housing & Dining Residence Life Households Off-Campus Housing Dining Student Opportunities Student Clubs Outreach Student Employment Baron Athletics Student Resources Student Handbook Wellness Center Connect Alumni & Giving Alumni News & Events Step in Faith Annual Fund Legacy Giving Ways to Give Why FUS? FUS at a Glance Mission, Vision, & Charism Our History Strategic Planning Resources Campus Map Campus Safety Travel and Accommodations News & Events University News Upcoming Events Franciscan Magazine Undergraduate Student Enrollment Checklist Welcome to Franciscan University of Steubenville Pay Your Deposit Not an incoming undergraduate student? Check out the enrollment process for Graduate & Online Students and International Students Deposit with Franciscan University Congratulations on your acceptance to Franciscan University of Steubenville! Ready to secure your spot at a top-ranked Catholic university? Submit your deposit, then follow the steps on this page to become a Baron. And don’t forget to celebrate! How to Submit Your Deposit Log in to your portal to make your nonrefundable $500 enrollment deposit. This will secure your place in the residence halls and classes, and it counts as a payment toward your first bill. While there is no deadline to submit your deposit, to guarantee your housing and roommate selection, you must submit your deposit and mutually request your roommate by May 1. Submit Your Deposit Next Steps for Admitted Students After you’ve submitted your deposit to officially save your spot at\n      Franciscan University, complete the action items in this checklist so\n      you’re ready to begin your first semester: 1. Set Up Your Port Account Your Port account gives you single sign-on access to your student email, your\n              financial aid awards and bill, helpful groups and information,\n              your class schedule, housing assignment, and more. Your preset username and password can be found in the email sent\n              to you when you were accepted. Contact your admissions counselor\n              if you need assistance. 2. Complete Your Financial Aid Steps Start by submitting the Free Application for Federal Student Aid (FAFSA®) using the school code 003036 to maximize the financial aid\n              available to you. Our Financial Aid Office will use your FAFSA\n              information to prepare your award package, including any grants,\n              scholarships, and other aid for which you qualify. Once your awards are available, visit the Finances & Accounts\n              tab in your Port account. Complete any required forms, including\n              the Master Promissory Note and Entrance Counseling if you are\n              accepting federal aid, and click Accept My Awards to accept your financial aid.\n              Your acceptance is nonbinding, and you can make adjustments before\n              paying your bill. Contact [email protected] or your admissions counselor with questions. 3. Select Your Housing and Meal Plan and Submit Your Health Forms If you have made your housing deposit, your next step is to\n              complete the New Student Housing Application via your Port account . This is where you will complete your roommate matching profile,\n              search for or request a roommate, and select your meal plan. We\n              encourage you to explore floor plans and photos of the residence\n              halls through the Resources tab on the Admissions page of your Port account. We’ll also need a completed Immunization Waiver Health form prior to Orientation. Additionally, there is a voluntary Health Record form that we strongly encourage you to complete so that it’s on file in\n              case of an emergency. Both forms can be found under the Applications tab in the\n              residence portal. Visit the Baron Health and Wellness Center page or contact [email protected] with any questions about our Health Center and Counseling\n              Services. 4. Check Your Class Schedule The Office of the Registrar will preregister you based on your\n              declared major and any transfer credits you have already earned.\n              Your schedule will be listed under the Academics tab on your Port account. It’s important that you fill out this advising questionnaire so we can put together the best schedule for you. If you have proficiency in a foreign language, you should also\n                take the Language Placement Exam before Orientation or early in your first year. The exam may\n                allow you to test out of some or all of the language\n                requirements. We encourage students in all majors to consider\n                taking it since your major may change. Contact [email protected] with questions, or meet with an academic advisor at Baron Day or\n              Orientation if you need to make changes to your schedule. 5. Submit Your Student ID Photo Before arriving on campus, upload a photo for your Franciscan\n                student ID. You’ll use your student ID to access your residence hall, use your\n              meal plan and make other purchases, and access printing services\n              on campus. You’ll receive your ID when you arrive. When uploading your photo, click the camera icon in your profile\n              to open the upload window. If you experience an error, make sure\n              only one pop-up window is open. 6. Send Your Final Transcripts Your final official transcripts must be on file with the Office\n              of Admissions before your first semester of classes begins, or\n              you will have a hold placed on your account and won’t be able to\n              register for the next semester until they are received. Send by mail to the Office of Admissions, or electronically to [email protected] . 7. Review and Pay Your Bill Visit the Finances & Accounts section of your Port account to\n              review your balance and access the Online Payment Center. From there, you can pay your balance, set up a payment plan, or\n              invite someone else to make payments on your account. Contact the Student Accounts Office at [email protected] with any questions about your bill or payment options. 8. Attend Baron Day Baron Day is a special event for students beginning in the fall\n                semester. Held before Orientation, it gives you the opportunity to become\n              familiar with campus, meet future classmates, connect with faculty\n              and staff, and complete important enrollment tasks before the\n              semester begins. Visit the Baron Day page for dates, registration, and additional\n                details. If you aren’t able to attend Baron Day, you’ll still have\n              opportunities to complete these tasks during Orientation. 9. Complete Your PathwayU Assessment PathwayU is a career discernment assessment that explores your\n              interests, values, personality, and workplace preferences to\n              identify careers that may be a good fit. We recommend completing the assessment before Baron Day or\n              Orientation so Career Services can help you begin exploring your\n              personal vocation and career goals. You can complete your PathwayU Assessment online here. For further questions, please contact [email protected] directly. 10. Review Your Textbooks New students are automatically enrolled in Franciscan’s Slingshot\n              textbook program. Your required textbooks are ordered based on\n              your class schedule and will update automatically if your schedule\n              changes. Before the semester begins, visit Slingshot to review your account , required materials, and available options. If you have questions about textbooks or your account, visit the\n                Slingshot support page. 11. Prepare for Move-In and Orientation Fall 2026 Move-in/Orientation is Thursday, August 20, 2026, with\n              Orientation programming continuing throughout the weekend.\n              Families are welcome to join! Review the orientation schedule , RSVP link, and further details, and if you have questions,\n              contact [email protected] . Get Connected & Discover Campus Life Explore Franciscan at Pre-Orientation RSVP for Baron Day Franciscan University Admitted Student FAQ Still have questions? We’re here to help. When is the Franciscan University deposit deadline? There is no formal deadline to deposit. However, accepted students\n              must submit their $500 enrollment deposit and housing and roommate\n              selection by May 1. Do I need to take any foreign language placement exams? Four semesters of a foreign language are required for all Bachelor\n              of Arts degrees, and the placement exam offers students with proficiency in a foreign language the\n              opportunity to test out of some or all of these requirements. We encourage all majors to take the exam before Orientation or\n              early in their first year. How does the roommate matching process work? You’ll use your Port account to fill out your roommate-matching profile and begin searching for\n              roommates. You can also find potential roommates by following @fusfall2026 on Instagram. Once you know who you’d like as your roommate(s), you’ll make your\n              roommate requests through your Port account by May 1 to guarantee placement. What should I pack for college? Review this packing list to learn more about what to bring—and what not to bring—to your\n              residence hall. When will I get my student ID? You’ll receive your ID upon arrival on campus and can use it to\n              access your residence hall, purchase meals, and print on campus. To begin the process of obtaining your ID, you may upload a photo for your card online . When uploading your photo, click on the camera icon in your\n              profile to enable a pop-up window. If you experience errors,\n              ensure that only one pop-up window is open at a time. What is PathwayU, and do I need to complete it? PathwayU is a career tool that uses a series of self-assessments\n              and a predictive matching algorithm to make recommendations for\n              paths that align with your interests, values, personality, and\n              workplace preferences. Watch this short video to learn more . We highly recommend completing PathwayU before Baron Day and Orientation so that you’re well positioned\n              for our Career Services team to guide you on your personal\n              vocation journey. How will I get my textbooks? Your textbooks will be automatically ordered via Slingshot in December for the spring semester or late July for the fall\n              semester, based on your class schedule. If you’re a new student, you’ll be automatically opted in to\n              Slingshot, unless you manually opt out before the semester. Your textbooks are hard-copy rentals by default. Any textbooks you\n              order ahead of time will be available for in-store pickup or\n              waiting in your residence hall when you arrive on campus. If your class schedule changes, your textbooks will automatically\n              change for you, and you can make free returns during the first\n              week of classes. How can I pay my tuition bill? You can set up a payment plan , send a payee invite, and pay your balance through your Online\n              Payment Center on the Financial Aid and Student Accounts page in your Port account. The fall bill or first payment is due July 15. The spring bill is\n              due December 15. Contact [email protected] with any questions. Tools for Success Academic Advising & Support Registrar Directory Questions? We’re here to help. We want to make securing your enrollment as stress-free as possible, so please reach out with any remaining questions. Undergraduate Admission Online Admission 740-283-6266 [email protected] About Franciscan Franciscan University of Steubenville is no ordinary university, and a Franciscan education is no ordinary education. Rather, it’s an education as rigorous and demanding as it is faithful—an education that challenges you intellectually, forms you professionally, and feeds you spiritually. Apply Inquire Visit Give Our Schools All Programs School of Humanities and Social Sciences School of Natural and Applied Sciences School of Online Programming School of Professional Programs School of Theology and Philosophy Franciscan Community Student Life Household Life Residence Life Student Clubs Athletics Alumni & Friends Steubenville Conferences Resources Career Opportunities The Port Visit, Amenities, & Directions Campus Store Campus Safety Title IX Resources Hazing Prevention Student Handbook Admissions Undergraduate Graduate Online Transfer International Scholarships Tuition & Financial Aid © Franciscan University of Steubenville Contact Us Privacy Policy Directions Nondiscrimination Statement Consumer Information Terms and Conditions Go to Top Apply Request Info Visit Programs Give"
    },
